* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   HOME
*
*   A start screen of tiles, in the toolkit's frame (140 columns by 43 lines at least; a smaller window is refused).  The administrator's page and an account
*   holder's page are built from one table and differ in what is in it; every tile starts a command exactly as the person would type it, so a tile adds no
*   access (the gates apply as at the terminal).
*
*     System     (the administrator only) Menu management, Bypass programs, Audit trail, Configuration
*     People and access, Data network and AI     (the administrator only) Accounts, Sessions and locks, Gates; Backups, Master files, Remote servers, AI.  Each tile starts the page's verb.
*     Develop    the development apps, for every account: Files (tool.files), Vocabulary (tool.voc), Dictionary (tool.dict), Queries (tool.query), Bulk update (tool.bulk), Programs (tool.catalog), Tests (tool.test)
*     Tools      the standard tools of every account: Editor (sed), IDE (ide)
*     Everyone   Terminal (leaves the screen for the ordinary prompt; EXIT comes back), Keys (tool.keys)
*     Header     who is signed on and when, Switch account (a pick list of the accounts this person may enter, then the screen of that account) and Sign out
*
*   Recent     (only when the person has turned it on, View menu) the tiles started last, newest first, as the first group of the page
*
*   SUB-MENUS.  A tile whose action is menu:<name> opens a page of its own: the groups of the table that belong to the menu <name> (the sixth value of a group, HG.MENU; the top page's
*   groups have none).  The page has a Back tile at the left of the header (the identity moves right), the status line shows the path (Home > Develop), Esc, Backspace and the Back tile go
*   back one level and give the focus and the scroll back as they were, 8 levels are the most, and a menu that is already open above is refused (no page can open itself).  A menu with no
*   tile (all turned off) has no folder tile.  No tile of the delivered page opens a sub-menu yet: a table that wants one declares a folder tile and gives its groups the menu name.
*
*   THE LAYOUT (S.88 L2) is the toolkit's layout service: three panes, the header (rows 1 to 4 at 140 by 43), the panel of tiles (rows 5 to 38) and the hint row (39, the AI line's
*   when the AI is on), declared at their 140 by 43 sizes; a bigger window gives the panel every row and column it has beyond that, and the header and the hint row every column.
*   The tile columns are worked out from the panel's width: two columns to a pair, one column between the two of a pair and three between pairs, as many as fit, and the columns
*   left over widen every tile alike (at 140 by 43 six columns of 21, at x 3 25 49 71 95 117).  A band with one group spreads it over every column; the groups of a band of several
*   share the columns beyond their own in whole pairs.  The header tiles stand on the last two columns, the Back tile on the first.  A resize is $tk_layout 'refresh', then all of it again.
*
*   The panel under the header scrolls: the page is laid out at its full height and the rows that fit are drawn, with a scroll bar in the last column.  Keys:
*   the arrow keys move to the nearest tile, Tab and Back-tab go in reading order, Home and End to the first and last, Page Up and Page Down by group, a letter
*   to the next tile that begins with it, / a window that finds a tile by any part of its name (typing narrows the list; Enter moves the focus to the tile), Enter or the space
*   bar starts the tile, F5 builds the page again, F6 the AI line, Ctrl-Q leaves to the prompt (signs out in a menu session or when the Terminal tile is off), Alt-T the Terminal (S.179; Ctrl-T is the theme), Ctrl-W Switch account, Ctrl-R the Recent group on or off, Ctrl-K Keys.
*   THE MENU BAR is the toolkit's ($tk_menubar): it is made from the key table (make.keys, then make.menus), so every entry is a key's action and runs through the key's case;
*   F10, Alt and a title's letter, a click on row 0, and Esc on the top page (the Esc rule: in a sub-menu Esc goes back) open it.  A click starts a tile, the wheel scrolls three
*   rows.  The end of the input ends the program.
*
*   THE AI LINE (only when the AI is on for the account: the gate, a provider, a model and a key; else there is no row and nothing below applies).  It takes the row that holds the
*   hint, after the last tile in the Tab ring (Tab from the last tile, Back-tab from the first), or a click.  Enter on a request shows everything that will be sent and sends nothing
*   until Send; the answer comes in a popup and may propose ONE of this page's tiles (Accept starts it as a click would; Enter alone accepts a tile that only opens a tool) or ONE
*   command (shown; it runs only on Run, as the Terminal would run it).  Sign out and Switch account are never offered.  The model sees the tile names, nothing of the account's data.
*
*   THE NAMED COMMON /TKHOME/: hflag is set when the person left by the Terminal tile (the EXIT verb then comes back to this screen); hlogto is set only
*   while this program runs a LOGTO, so that the sign-on hook (!home_entry) does not start a second screen inside it.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $tool_home
$catalog $tool_home

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include err.h
$include sl.h
$include tk.h

deffun is_grp_member(user, group) calling '!is_grp_member'

$define HT.ID      1       ;* a tile is a field of tl, its values:  the id (for the key jump and the tests)
$define HT.GROUP   2       ;* the group's number in gr
$define HT.LABEL   3
$define HT.GLYPH   4       ;* three cells of ASCII, so that any locale draws it
$define HT.SUB     5       ;* the line under the label
$define HT.ACT     6       ;* cmd:<sentence>, terminal, switch or signout
$define HT.PAL     7       ;* the palette's name
$define HT.SIZE    8       ;* n normal 21 by 5, w wide 43 by 5, s small 21 by 3 (the widths at 140 by 43: one column, two columns and the gap between them, one column)
$define HT.PAUSE   9       ;* 1 when the command prints lines: wait for a key before the screen comes back
$define HT.WHY     10      ;* null, or why the tile is dimmed (a gate that is off)
$define HT.X       11      ;* the cell of its top left corner: x; y is a row of the panel for a tile in a group and a row of the screen for a header tile (both set by the layout)
$define HT.Y       12
$define HT.W       13
$define HT.H       14
$define HT.FIXED   15      ;* 1 for a header tile (not scrolled)
$define HT.LIVE    16      ;* null, or the live line shown in place of the sub line (read again when the page opens, on F5 and every 30 seconds)

$define HG.NAME    1       ;* a group is a field of gr, its values
$define HG.BAND    2       ;* the band it stands in: groups of one band stand side by side
$define HG.COLS    3       ;* how many tile columns it is wide at 140 by 43 (2 or 6); a wider panel gives it more (band.cols)
$define HG.Y       4       ;* the panel row of its heading, set by the layout
$define HG.X       5
$define HG.MENU    6       ;* null for a group of the top page, else the name of the menu (sub-menu page) it belongs to

$define HT.TW      21      ;* a tile's width in the narrowest panel (138 columns); a wider panel widens every tile by what is left over after the columns that fit
$define HT.GAP     1       ;* the columns between the two tiles of a pair of columns
$define HT.PGAP    3       ;* the columns between two pairs
$define HT.BASECOLS 6      ;* the tile columns of the 140 by 43 panel: a panel with no more than these gives every group exactly its HG.COLS (band.cols), as the page was drawn before the service
$define HM.DEPTH   8       ;* sub-menu pages open at most this many levels deep (MAX.DEPTH is int$keys.h's)

$define H.TILE.OFF 'turned off for this account by the administrator'     ;* why a key and its menu entry cannot be used when the tile is off (S.88 U9)
$define H.AI.OFF   'the AI is not on for this account'                    ;* why F6 and AI > Ask the AI do nothing here
$define H.TOP.PAGE 'this is the top page'                                 ;* why Go > Back cannot be chosen on the top page

   common /tkhome/ hflag, hlogto, hstart, hrefused     ;* S.164: hrefused is set when the screen could not open, for !home_entry to sign the session out

   prompt ''

   if @tty[1, 5] # '/dev/' then
      display sysmsg(12868)                             ;* no terminal: nothing here could be read as keys
      stop
   end
   hlogto = 0
   menu.session = hstart + 0                           ;* 3 Nov 26: 1 when !home_entry (the account starts in the menu) or EXIT (back from the Terminal tile) started this screen, 0 when the person typed HOME at the prompt
   hstart = 0                                           ;* taken: a later HOME typed at the prompt the screen gave is typed

   call $tk_open('home', '', ctx, err)
   if err # '' then
      hrefused = 1                                      ;* S.164: read by !home_entry after the EXECUTE
      display err
      stop
   end
   gosub declare.panes
   if perr # '' then
      call $tk_close(ctx)
      display perr
      stop
   end

   is.admin = kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)
   if is.admin then ctx<TK.C.AUD> = sysmsg(12808)

   cur.menu = ''                                          ;* the sub-menu page being shown (null: the top page); stk holds the pages left behind, crumb their names
   stk = ''
   crumb = ''
   gosub geometry
   gosub set.title                                        ;* the path, and the column of the identity (it moves right of the Back tile in a sub-menu)
   * 3 Nov 26 (owner: "make ctrl+q leave for the prompt when you typed home"): a screen typed at the prompt is left for the prompt by Ctrl-Q, and Sign out moves to Ctrl-X, the menu and the tile;
   * a screen the session started in (or came back to by EXIT) keeps Ctrl-Q as Sign out.  An account whose Terminal tile is off has no prompt to leave for: Ctrl-Q signs out, as before.
   call !home_off(@who, off.list)
   leave.ok = (not(menu.session) and not(index(' ' : off.list : ' ', ' terminal ', 1)))
   gosub make.keys

   gen = 0
   top = 0
   fi = 1
   msg = ''
   outcome = ''
   done = 0
   lastmin = ''
   keep.id = ''
   keep.top = 0
   rc.on = 0                                              ;* 1 when the Recent group is on for this page (set by add.recent)

   aif = 0                                                ;* 1 while the AI line has the focus
   call $tk_ai('init', ctx, '', '')

   gosub build.page
   gosub layout
   gosub first.focus
   gosub draw.all

   loop
      call $tk_menubar('draw', ctx, keys, ttl, '', '', mr)  ;* the bar first: it writes row 0 whole, and the frame then writes the app name at its right end
      call $tk_frame(ctx, keys, ttl, msg)
      if aif then
         call $tk_ai('draw', ctx, 1, '')                     ;* the cursor is in the AI line while it has the focus
      end else
         call $sl_curat(0, ctx<TK.C.YSTAT>)                  ;* the cursor waits at the start of the status line: in a window made smaller it is off the screen, and the library's cursor lock takes the keyboard
      end
      nm = ''
      screen key timeout 30 returning nm
      mr = ''
      if nm # '' and not(aif) then
         busy = (cur.menu # '')                           ;* the Esc rule: in a sub-menu Esc is the page's (back one page; the key table binds it), on the top page the bar's
         call $tk_menubar('key', ctx, keys, ttl, nm, busy, mr)     ;* F10, Alt and a letter, Esc on the top page: the bar; a choice comes back as the key it has
      end
      if mr<1> # '' then nm = mr<4>
      begin case
         case nm = ''
            if status() = ER$EOF then exit
            gosub draw.clock
            gosub live.draw
         case nm = 'guardquit'
            exit                                          ;* the cursor lock's own way out
         case nm = 'resize'
            gosub relayout
         case nm = 'mouse'
            gosub on.mouse
         case 1
            gosub on.key
      end case
      if done then exit
   repeat

   gosub finish
   stop

* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- how the program ends

finish:
   begin case
      case terminal.off and outcome # 'signout'
         * the Terminal tile is off for this account: the screen is not left for the prompt, whatever ended it (the cursor lock, the end of the input, a window too small
         * to take the screen again); the person is signed out
         if outcome = 'closed' then
            sleep 2                                       ;* the message that said why is on the screen; let it be read
         end else
            call $tk_close(ctx)
         end
         execute 'quit'
      case outcome = 'closed'
         null                                             ;* the screen was given back already (it could not be taken again)
      case outcome = 'terminal'
         call $tk_close(ctx)
         hflag = 1                                        ;* EXIT comes back here
         display sysmsg(12869)
      case outcome = 'signout'
         call $tk_close(ctx)
         execute 'quit'
      case outcome = 'leave'
         call $tk_close(ctx)                              ;* typed at the prompt, so the prompt it was typed at is where Ctrl-Q goes back to (hflag stays as it was: EXIT still ends the session)
      case 1
         call $tk_close(ctx)
   end case
   return

* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the geometry and the page

* The page as panes of the layout service ($tk_layout, S.88 L2), each at its size in the 140 by 43 layout (body 138 by 39 at 1,1): the header (rows 1 to 4: the identity, the
* clock and the header tiles on rows 1 to 3), the panel of tiles (rows 5 to 38) and the hint row (39, YSTAT - 1: the AI line's row when the AI is on).  The panel takes every row
* and column a bigger window has beyond that; the header and the hint row take every column.  perr returns null, or why the service refused the declaration.
declare.panes:
   pd = ''
   pd<1> = 'header' : @vm : 1 : @vm : 1 : @vm : 4 : @vm : 138 : @vm : '' : @vm : '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 'stretch' : @vm : 1
   pd<2> = 'panel' : @vm : 2 : @vm : 1 : @vm : 34 : @vm : 138 : @vm : '' : @vm : 'stretch' : @vm : 1 : @vm : 'stretch' : @vm : 1
   pd<3> = 'hint' : @vm : 3 : @vm : 1 : @vm : 1 : @vm : 138 : @vm : '' : @vm : '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 'stretch' : @vm : 1
   perr = ''
   call $tk_layout('declare', ctx, pd, perr)
   return

* The rectangles the layout service gives the three panes now (the layout is the window when it is bigger than the floor, the floor when it is smaller), and the tile columns
* of the panel's width.  lw and lh are the layout's; py0 to py1 are the panel's rows (ph of them); the hint row is hny.
geometry:
   lw = ctx<TK.C.W>
   lh = ctx<TK.C.H>
   call $tk_layout('pane', ctx, 'header', prect)
   hdx = prect<1, 1>
   hdy = prect<1, 2>
   hdw = prect<1, 3>
   call $tk_layout('pane', ctx, 'panel', prect)
   pnx = prect<1, 1>
   py0 = prect<1, 2>
   pnw = prect<1, 3>
   ph = prect<1, 4>
   py1 = py0 + ph - 1
   call $tk_layout('pane', ctx, 'hint', prect)
   hnx = prect<1, 1>
   hny = prect<1, 2>
   gosub columns
   return

* The tile columns of a panel pnw wide: the first two columns in from the panel's left edge, two columns to a pair with HT.GAP between them and HT.PGAP between pairs, as many as
* fit with the last one ending a column short of the panel's right edge (the scroll bar is the column after the panel), and the columns left over shared out to every tile alike
* (tw.n wide).  At 140 by 43 (panel 138) that is six columns of 21 at x 3 25 49 71 95 117: the page as it was drawn before the service.
columns:
   avail = pnw - 3
   ncol = 1
   loop
      nx = ncol + 1
      need = nx * HT.TW + (nx - 1) * HT.GAP + idiv(nx - 1, 2) * (HT.PGAP - HT.GAP)
   while need <= avail do
      ncol = nx
   repeat
   used = ncol * HT.TW + (ncol - 1) * HT.GAP + idiv(ncol - 1, 2) * (HT.PGAP - HT.GAP)
   tw.n = HT.TW + idiv(avail - used, ncol)
   xcol = ''
   for k = 1 to ncol
      xcol<k> = pnx + 2 + (k - 1) * (tw.n + HT.GAP) + idiv(k - 1, 2) * (HT.PGAP - HT.GAP)
   next k
   return

* The tiles of this page.  The table is built for the audience (13.2): the administrator gets the administrator's, everyone else the holder's, and what a person may
* not use is not in memory.  Later apps add their rows here, in the commit that builds them.
build.page:
   * the tiles the administrator has turned off for this account (SET.TILE): left out of the page, their menu entries cannot be chosen
   call !home_off(@who, off.list)
   off.set = ' ' : off.list : ' '
   terminal.off = (index(off.set, ' terminal ', 1) > 0)
   tl = ''
   gr = ''
   pal.tools = (if is.admin then 'plum' else 'green')
   gname = (if is.admin then 'Tools' else 'My tools')
   bnd = 0                                                ;* the groups stand one band under another; a group that is not there takes no band
   g = 0
   if is.admin then
      * the administrator's own group: the page that manages what every account's home screen shows (it is in SDSYS's VOC only; an account holder's page has no such tile)
      bnd += 1
      g += 1
      gr<g> = 'System' : @vm : bnd : @vm : 2                 ;* the administrator's three groups stand side by side in one band, two tiles wide each, so that the page needs no scrolling
      id = 'menus' ; grp = g ; label = 'Menu management' ; glyph = '[=]' ; sub = 'tiles and start' ; act = 'cmd:tool.menus' ; pal = 'rust' ; size = 'n' ; pause = 0 ; why = '' ; gosub add.tile
      id = 'audit' ; grp = g ; label = 'Audit trail' ; glyph = '[*]' ; sub = 'who did what' ; act = 'cmd:tool.audit' ; pal = 'rust' ; size = 'n' ; pause = 0 ; why = '' ; gosub add.tile
      id = 'config' ; grp = g ; label = 'Configuration' ; glyph = '[%]' ; sub = 'sd.conf and SD' ; act = 'cmd:tool.config' ; pal = 'rust' ; size = 'n' ; pause = 0 ; why = '' ; gosub add.tile
      * who may do what, and who is on: the people and access group
      g += 1
      gr<g> = 'People and access' : @vm : bnd : @vm : 2
      id = 'accounts' ; grp = g ; label = 'Accounts' ; glyph = '(@)' ; sub = 'create and manage' ; act = 'cmd:tool.accounts' ; pal = 'amber' ; size = 'n' ; pause = 0 ; why = '' ; gosub add.tile
      id = 'sessions' ; grp = g ; label = 'Sessions and locks' ; glyph = '[~]' ; sub = 'who is on' ; act = 'cmd:tool.sessions' ; pal = 'amber' ; size = 'n' ; pause = 0 ; why = '' ; gosub add.tile
      id = 'gates' ; grp = g ; label = 'Gates' ; glyph = '[#]' ; sub = 'who may do what' ; act = 'cmd:tool.gates' ; pal = 'amber' ; size = 'n' ; pause = 0 ; why = '' ; gosub add.tile
